Z||000000||Adrift||10-16-2001||07:35 PM||||Do speaker pods have a back end to them, or do they just use the material on your door for the "back"? (Do you need a back piece made of wood or something for reverb or something?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Philphine||10-16-2001||08:05 PM||||the pods i've made for my fieros and other cars i put backs on (except one set, but i want to seal them up too). i think it's supposed to help with cone movement of the woofer (midrange, midbass, however you want to term it). also you don't want the back wave (think sound coming off the back of the speaker) as part of what you're hearing. guess you'd have to have it sealed against the panel to get the same results if you don't put a back on it.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||10-17-2001||08:30 AM||||The ones I bought off of AusFiero have a back with a hole cut out for the speaker magnet.  More for strength than anything else, I think.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||10-17-2001||08:30 AM||||I saw some vacuum molded speaker enclosures at PepBoys (kind of looked like a plastic pie plate).  These are meant to give weather protection to the back side of the speaker and to help in sound dispersion (or something like that).  The come in sizes to fit varoius speakers and cost around $12.00 US.||||reg||1
